---
<PageLayout
  title="How We Test and Choose Car Gear"
  heading="How we test"
  description="How DriveTested researches, tests and chooses the car accessories we recommend, and how we label research-based picks."
  lede="Our recommendations follow the same process every time, and every guide tells you which parts are hands-on and which are research."
>
  <h2>1. Research first</h2>
  <p>
    For every category we start by building a long list of products that are actually available to buy. We read
    manufacturer specifications and manuals, look at warranty terms, check safety certifications where they matter,
    and read published independent test results from established outlets. We also read the long tail of owner reviews
    to find recurring failures that only show up after months of use.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Hands-on testing</h2>
  <p>
    Products that make our shortlist are tested in normal use with our own vehicles. Our testing is designed around
    the jobs you actually need the gear to do, for example:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Jump starters:</strong> starting a vehicle with a deliberately discharged battery, checking clamp grip and cable reach, and confirming the safety protections (reverse polarity, short circuit) behave as described.</li>
    <li><strong>Tire inflators and compressors:</strong> timing how long it takes to top up a tire by a set amount, and comparing the device's built-in gauge with a separate digital tire gauge.</li>
    <li><strong>Dash cams:</strong> reviewing day, night and glare footage, and checking whether plates are readable.</li>
    <li><strong>Floor mats and seat covers:</strong> checking fit, edge coverage and how well they hold up to water, mud and daily wear.</li>
  </ul>
  {/* TODO OWNER: add your test vehicles and tools here once confirmed, e.g. "We test in a 20XX Make Model and a 20XX Make Model." */}

  <h2>3. Clear labels: tested vs. researched</h2>
  <p>
    Some guides are published before every product in them has been through hands-on testing. When that's the case,
    we say so near the top of the article and explain what the recommendations are based on. When we add our own
    test results, we update the guide and the "Updated" date.
  </p>

  <h2>4. How we choose winners</h2>
  <p>
    We weigh real-world performance, safety, ease of use, build quality, warranty and support, and value. A product
    doesn't need the biggest numbers on the box to win; it needs to do the job reliably for the person it's meant for.
  </p>
  <h3>What the score out of 10 means</h3>
  <p>
    Each pick in our buying guides shows a <strong>DriveTested score out of 10</strong>. Until we've tested a product
    ourselves, that score is <strong>research-based</strong>: it combines published results from independent testers,
    the manufacturer's specifications and manuals, warranty length and support, and patterns we see across owner
    reviews (especially failures that show up after months of use). Scores compare products within the same guide, for
    the job that guide covers. They aren't hands-on ratings, and we label them that way on every page. When we add our own
    test results, we update the score and say so.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Independence</h2>
  <p>
    We don't accept payment for placement, and brands don't review or approve our articles before they're published.
    We may earn a commission when you buy through our links. That's how the site is funded, and it doesn't affect our
    picks. See our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Keeping guides current</h2>
  <p>
    Products change, get discontinued, or get replaced by better options. We review our guides regularly and update
    them when the recommendation changes. We don't show prices on the page because they change too often. Use the
    "Check price on Amazon" buttons to see the current price.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
